3	// Signing in to a JusPrin account, which is how the "jusprin" provider gets
4	// its credential: an OAuth2 access token from the JusPrin server, used as the
5	// gateway's bearer token where another provider takes an API key.
6	//
7	// Sign-in is the authorization code flow with PKCE in the system browser,
8	// answered on a loopback port (RFC 8252); the server accepts any port on its
9	// http://127.0.0.1/callback redirect. Or it stays in the app: the person
10	// types an address and picks a code the server emails or the account's
11	// password. The server's auth endpoints are django-allauth's API for apps,
12	// and answer either with the same kind of token. Finishing a sign-in, and checking a
13	// token the app already holds (one imported from JusPrin v1, or the stored
14	// one), are the same step: ask the server who the account is and which
15	// models it serves this account, in which wire format. A token that passes
16	// that step is stored with the answers, and the agent built from it is handed
17	// over the way key setup hands over its verified agent.
18	//
19	// GUI-free and poll-driven like ProviderKeySetup: the browser, the loopback
20	// server and HTTP are injected, their callbacks may arrive on any thread and
21	// only queue, and outcomes are released from poll() on the owner's thread.
22	
